 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Memory Efficiency:** By storing only non-transparent bricks, the method reduces the memory footprint compared to storing the full volume [S2, unofficial].
-- **Bandwidth:** Bricking can suffer from bus-bandwidth problems and unbalanced load for the GPU and memory bus, making it inefficient if not managed carefully [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Keeping bricks small enough ensures more than one fits in video memory, allowing parallel transfer and rendering to balance the load, though this increases CPU load for intersection calculations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Complexity:** Requires manual handling of filtering and interpolation due to the packed nature of the texture, increasing shader complexity [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of samples per ray and the number of bricks. The index lookup adds a small overhead.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of non-transparent bricks. Transparent bricks are not stored, saving memory [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Efficient for sparse volumes. For dense volumes, the overhead of index lookups and potential cache misses may reduce performance [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Handles large datasets that exceed GPU memory. Efficient storage for sparse data.
+- **Limitations:** Bus-bandwidth bottlenecks. Unbalanced load for GPU and memory bus. Artifacts at brick boundaries if not handled correctly [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** Large volumetric datasets with significant empty space or transparency.
+- **When Not to Use:** Dense volumes where most bricks are non-transparent, or when high-quality interpolation across brick boundaries is critical and cannot be achieved with duplicated boundary voxels.
 
 ## Background
-This method is a variation of bricking, which addresses the problem of volumetric data sets being too large to fit into local video memory [S1, unofficial]. It was discussed in the context of GPU-based volume rendering and online forums for implementing large dataset visualization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+Bricking was introduced as a technique to handle data sets too large for local video memory by dividing the data set into smaller chunks (bricks) [S1, unofficial]. The specific use of packed textures and index textures for volume rendering was discussed in community forums and presentations on GPU-based volume rendering [S2, unofficial][S1, unofficial]. The technique has been used in scientific visualization and real-time rendering applications [S3, unofficial].
 
